<attribute name="refinedDescription" nodeId="4d8b86fb">&lt;p&gt;
    A reference model is an abstract framework for understanding significant relationships among the entities of an
    environment. It enables the development of specific reference or concrete architectures using consistent standards or
    specifications supporting that environment. A reference model consists of a minimal set of unifying concepts, axioms
    and relationships within a particular problem domain, and is independent of specific standards, technologies,
    implementations, or other concrete details.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    A reference model has certain key principles &lt;sup&gt;[1] [2]&lt;/sup&gt;:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Abstract&lt;/strong&gt; - reference model describes the type of entities that may occur in an environment
        and not the actual entities. For example, when describing an office building, a smart
        card based entrance door can have specifications and characteristics but the concept of secured
        entrance (type of entity) is part of a reference model for that environment.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Entities and relationships&lt;/strong&gt; - reference model should define entities and relationships with each
        other. When only entities are listed, the relationships are neglected and the reference model and
        its derived architectures as such are less useful. 
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Within a particular problem domain&lt;/strong&gt; - reference model should have a clearly focused
        problem area for a specific environment.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Technology agnostic&lt;/strong&gt; - reference model should not make assumptions about specific technology or
        standard implementation.  
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;sup&gt;[1]&lt;/sup&gt; OASIS Reference Model for SOA, Version 1.0, OASIS Standard, October 2006
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;sup&gt;[2]&lt;/sup&gt; Morris Riedel, Design and Applications of an Interoperability Reference Model for Production e-Science
    Infrastructures, July 2012
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
</Element>
</reference>
